    "reading_order": 18,
    "text": "We now investigate the notion of the converse of a statement. For this, we need the\nnotion of a ‘compound’ statement, that is, a statement which is a combination of one or\nmore ‘simple’ statements. There are many ways of creating compound statements,\nbut we will focus on those that are created by connecting two simple statements with\nthe use of the words ‘if’ and ‘then’. For example, the statement ‘If it is raining, then it\nis difficult to go on a bicycle’, is made up of two statements:",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
