      48.02,
      530.41,
      420.1,
      612.15
    ],
    "reading_order": 22,
    "text": "Now, supose you have the statement ‘If the water tank is black, then it contains\npotable water.’ This is of the form p  q, where the hypothesis is p (the water tank\nis black) and the conclusion is q (the tank contains potable water). Suppose we\ninterchange  the hypothesis and the conclusion, what do we get? We get q  p, i.e., if\nthe water in the tank is potable, then the tank must be black. This statement is called\nthe converse of the statement p  q.",
    "latex": null,
